# tools/entity-census-patterns -- the §3.16 cast families, one per line.
#
# FORMAT: <name><TAB><extended regex>.  Comments start with '#', blank lines
# are ignored.  Names are the row labels in the census; the regexes go to
# `grep -E` verbatim.
#
# READ THIS FILE WITH `read -r`.  Without -r, `read` treats a backslash as a
# line-continuation/escape introducer and EATS IT: `\(` becomes `(`, `\s`
# becomes `s`, `\b` becomes `b`.  Every regex here then means something else
# and every count comes out wrong -- which is not hypothetical, it is what
# happened the first time this census was taken (plan_entity.md Appendix A
# records it).  The failure is silent: `grep -E '( *\* *)'` is a valid regex,
# it just matches almost nothing.  entity_census_test.cpp holds a fact that a
# backslash survives the round trip through the loader, so a regression in
# the loader is a red test rather than a quietly smaller number.
#
# A NEW SPELLING OF THE SAME CAST IS A LINE HERE, NOT A HOLE.  §3.16's gate is
# only as wide as this list.

# THE RULINGS THE CLOSE MADE, AND WHY EACH ONE IS NOT A HOLE
# ==========================================================
#
# Five of these rows changed when the port finished. Three NARROWED, one
# WIDENED, and the widening is the one that found work: adding `IGameEntity` and
# an optional `const` to the typed families turned up EIGHT cast sites the old
# regexes walked straight past, including a `(C_StandaloneEntity *)` on a
# slot-table Resolve that was the allowlisted resolver written out by hand.
#
# ROUTE_TO_DONE §4(d) asked for one of these in as many words -- "the EHANDLE
# row ... needs a ruling; `CHandle<IGameEntity>` is the design's own handle" --
# and the rest are the same question asked of the neighbouring rows.
#
# THE RULE BEHIND ALL FIVE: this file counts CONVERSIONS THAT ASSUME A LAYOUT.
# A conversion that ASKS -- a virtual call, a serial-checked table lookup, a
# specialisation written for exactly this -- is not one, whatever it is spelled.
# An UPCAST is not one either; the compiler does it and can see the whole class.
#
#   1. TYPED, not bare: `dynamic_cast` and `assert_cast` used to match ANY
#      target. In a finished port what is left under those spellings is
#      `dynamic_cast<Label *>( FindChildByName(...) )` in the scoreboard,
#      `<vgui::ComboBox *>` in the spectator GUI, `<CNewParticleEffect *>` in
#      the leaf system and `<CClientSystemPerFrame *>` in the client-system
#      walk -- eleven sites, not one of them an entity. Counting them made the
#      gate unreachable for a reason that is not entity coupling, which is the
#      one failure mode §5 warns about by name. They now name the same entity
#      types the other cast families already name, so a dynamic_cast ONTO an
#      entity is still a site and a vgui panel is not.
#
#   2. EHANDLE: the row's subject, in §3.16's own words, is that
#      "`CHandle<T>::Get()` is `(T*)CBaseHandle::Get()` ... correct only when
#      `T`'s `IHandleEntity` is at offset zero". That is exactly what
#      `CHandle<IGameEntity>` is NOT: BOTH halves are specialised --
#      `Get()` in game/server/baseentity.h and game/client/c_baseentity.h
#      (`SourceSharp_CrossHandleToGameEntity`, a virtual call), `Set()` in
#      game/shared/ehandle.h (a scan of the entity list) -- precisely because
#      IGameEntity is a SEPARATE base sitting 24 measured bytes from the handle
#      view. So `CHandle<IGameEntity>`, spelled EHANDLE, is the design's handle
#      and not the cast this row hunts. The row now names `CHandle<` of a STOCK
#      entity class, which is the shape that still would be one.
#      PINNED: close_rulings_test.cpp fails if either specialisation
#      stops existing or stops asking.
#
#   3. EntityFromHandle: `EntityFromEntityHandle` and `TraceEntity` are DELETED
#      from the tree (the close, 4/N: every GetBaseEntity() in both DLLs is a
#      constant NULL, so they could only answer NULL). The row keeps naming them
#      so that re-introducing one is a red gate. `GameEntityFromEntityHandle`
#      and `TraceGameEntity`, which call the virtual `GetGameEntity()` and
#      answer `IGameEntity *`, come out: they are §3.16 family 3's own
#      replacement -- "the pair of functions per question, one per hierarchy,
#      collapses to one" -- and the one that survived is the one that asks.
#
#   4. WIDENED: every typed family now also names `IGameEntity` and tolerates a
#      leading `const`. `static_cast< const C_StandaloneEntity * >` was invisible
#      to this file until now, and so was every cast onto the port's own entity
#      interface. THIS IS THE ROW THAT CAN STILL FIRE: the stock entity classes
#      are gone from the tree, so the rows naming them are tripwires against
#      re-introduction, while the `IGameEntity` half is a live check on the
#      hierarchy that actually exists.
#
# THREE ROWS ARE LEFT EXACTLY AS THEY WERE and read 0 for the same reason --
# `Instance`, `GetContainingEntity` and `GetGameData` name things this tree no
# longer contains. They are tripwires and are described as such rather than
# deleted, because a deleted row is a hole and a row at 0 is a check.

cstyle	\(\s*(const\s+)?(C_?Base(Entity|Player|Animating|AnimatingOverlay|CombatCharacter|CombatWeapon|Flex)|CWorld|C_World|CStandalone[A-Za-z]*|C_Standalone[A-Za-z]*|IGameEntity)\s*\*\s*\)
static_cast	static_cast\s*<\s*(const\s+)?(C_?Base(Entity|Player|Animating|AnimatingOverlay|CombatCharacter|CombatWeapon|Flex)|CWorld|C_World|CStandalone[A-Za-z]*|C_Standalone[A-Za-z]*|IGameEntity)\s*\*
reinterpret_cast	reinterpret_cast\s*<\s*(const\s+)?(C_?Base(Entity|Player|Animating|AnimatingOverlay|CombatCharacter|CombatWeapon|Flex)|CWorld|C_World|CStandalone[A-Za-z]*|C_Standalone[A-Za-z]*|IGameEntity)\s*\*
assert_cast	assert_cast\s*<\s*(const\s+)?(C_?Base(Entity|Player|Animating|AnimatingOverlay|CombatCharacter|CombatWeapon|Flex)|CWorld|C_World|CStandalone[A-Za-z]*|C_Standalone[A-Za-z]*|IGameEntity)\s*\*
dynamic_cast	dynamic_cast\s*<\s*(const\s+)?(C_?Base(Entity|Player|Animating|AnimatingOverlay|CombatCharacter|CombatWeapon|Flex)|CWorld|C_World|CStandalone[A-Za-z]*|C_Standalone[A-Za-z]*|IGameEntity)\s*\*
ToBaseX	\bTo(BasePlayer|BaseCombatCharacter|BaseAnimating|BaseCombatWeapon|BaseFlex)\s*\(
Instance	CBaseEntity::Instance\s*\(
EntityFromHandle	\b(EntityFromEntityHandle|TraceEntity)\s*\(
GetBaseEntity	->GetBaseEntity\s*\(
GetContainingEntity	\bGetContainingEntity\s*\(
GetGameData	GetGameData\s*\(\s*\)
EHANDLE	\bCHandle\s*<\s*(const\s+)?(C_?Base(Entity|Player|Animating|AnimatingOverlay|CombatCharacter|CombatWeapon|Flex)|CWorld|C_World)\s*>
# ENTITY -> ENGINE INTERFACE, the direction §3.16's table does not cover.
#
# The rows above count casts TO an entity. This one counts the explicit casts
# the other way: an entity pointer forced to IHandleEntity, IServerEntity,
# IClientRenderable and the rest of the bases CBaseEntity/C_BaseEntity have
# TODAY. It exists because tools/cast-audit -- which had this bug class to
# itself -- was deleted at b6c429e1, and because the class is invisible to a
# compiler: while the base relationship holds, the cast is an ordinary
# derived->base conversion (with a NON-ZERO pointer adjustment for the client's
# secondary bases); the moment the base goes away, the very same C-style cast
# falls back to reinterpret_cast, silently, at every -W level, and the pointer
# is wrong at runtime. This plan removes those bases, so every site here is one
# that has to be rewritten rather than recompiled.
ToEngineIface	(\(\s*(const\s+)?(IHandleEntity|IServerUnknown|IServerEntity|IServerNetworkable|ICollideable|IClientUnknown|IClientEntity|IClientNetworkable|IClientRenderable|IClientThinkable|IGameEntity)\s*\*\s*\)|(static_cast|reinterpret_cast)\s*<\s*(const\s+)?(IHandleEntity|IServerUnknown|IServerEntity|IServerNetworkable|ICollideable|IClientUnknown|IClientEntity|IClientNetworkable|IClientRenderable|IClientThinkable|IGameEntity)\s*\*)
